Rotation function:

```javascript
function rotateMatrix(matrix) {
    const n = matrix.length;
    const result = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        for (let j = 0; j < n; j++) {
            result[j][n-1-i] = matrix[i][j];
        }
    }
    return result;
}
```

This rotates 90° clockwise.

Now, for each piece type, we can generate all rotations by applying rotateMatrix up to 3 times. But note that some pieces have symmetry, so we might get duplicates. We'll just store all 4 rotations (some may be same). For simplicity, we can store the four rotations as arrays of matrices.

Alternatively, we can store the base shape and compute rotation on the fly when needed, but we need to know the current rotation index for each piece. We'll maintain a rotation index (0-3) for each piece instance. When rotating, we increment index modulo 4 and get the corresponding matrix.

For the preview piece, we also need to rotate automatically. So we need to keep track of its rotation index and animate.

